<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perkūnas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Perkūnas was the thunder god of the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and the most important deity of the Baltic religion, corresponding to the Latvian Pērkons. Associated with oak trees, rain and fertility, he was worshipped until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> from 1387. He survives in Lithuanian folklore and in the word for thunder.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perkunas.jpg" alt="Perkūnas" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Society for the Protection of Monuments and Local History of the Lithuanian SSR (Lietuvos TSR paminklų apsaugos ir kr... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perkunas_of_Kernav%C4%97s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
